WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.670
Hey Crazies. Say you've got a bowling ball in a boat.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.907
As you look deep into the water beneath, the darkness beckons.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:12.278
In your soul you feel an overwhelming urge to drop the ball.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:15.715
Does the water level go up? Go down? Or stay the same?

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:19.352
We'll get into it after the intro. Feel free to pause and think if you need to.

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:27.727
(Off Screen) Why would we have a bowling ball in a boat?
Who doesn't have a bowling ball in their boat?

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:31.664
Anyway, I used to frequently give this question to my Intro Physics students,

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.268
and their most common response was that the water level would go up.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:38.304
They’d say objects in water displace that water.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:43.576
So putting a bowling ball into water would displace it like anything else, raising the water level.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:48.415
But as it turns out, the water level can't go up if nothing enters the system.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:53.353
The bowling ball is already in the system, so that only leaves us with two options.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:56.623
Either the water level stays the same or it goes down.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.661
It stays the same, doesn’t it?
Actually, it depends on whether the bowling ball sinks or floats.

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:03.730
Bowling balls can float?!
Yep.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:09.302
Any bowling ball under about 12 pounds or 5.5 kg will float on water.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.071
All the heavier ones sink.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.806
Let's take a look at the floaters first.

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:17.911
[Laughing]
(Off Screen) Poop jokes.
Poop jokes.

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:22.082
(Off Screen) You’re such a dork.

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:25.185
Here we have an analog for each item in our system.

00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:27.854
First, we'll fill up our “lake” with water.

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.722
It's not a lake without water.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:34.294
Then our system begins with the boat in the water and the ball in the boat.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.197
We should mark the water level before we change anything.

00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:38.765
This is our new baseline.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:43.703
Then we take the ball out, carefully place it in the water, and wait for everything to settle.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:47.173
Checking the water level again shows that it hasn't changed.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:50.977
Which makes sense if you think about it. The ball is floating regardless.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:55.582
Afterward, it's floating on its own. But beforehand, it's using the boat to float.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:58.485
The water is still supporting the weight either way.

00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:01.054
And you can see that when you work out the math, too.

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:04.023
The “before” picture looks something like this.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:07.227
Everything is in what we call “mechanical equilibrium,”

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.729
meaning the forces all balance each other.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:14.801
In this case, gravity pulls downward on the boat and the ball. That's their weight.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.873
There's also upward support from the water, which we call “buoyancy” or “buoyant force.”

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:23.276
That buoyancy comes from the weight of the water itself.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:29.382
When you put something like a boat into water, it takes up space that used to be occupied by water.

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:34.654
That water got pushed out of the way and ultimately upward above the original waterline.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:40.160
But water also has weight and it's trying to fall toward the earth just like everything else.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.864
In the process that water pushes back, resulting in an upward force on the boat.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:49.669
The two find an equilibrium, though, and this new water level becomes our new baseline.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.905
Now let's take a look at the “after” picture.

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:55.942
When the ball is lifted out of the boat, the water level goes down.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:58.878
But then it goes back up when the ball is placed in the water.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.382
By the same exact amount?
In this case, yes.

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:07.287
We have two objects independently in equilibrium, but the physics works out the same.

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:10.023
Each gets a buoyancy and each gets a weight.

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:14.060
Adding everything together, we recover the same result we got before.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.798
Even though the displaced volume is a different shape, it's still the same amount of volume.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:22.602
So, what happens if you drop a 16 pound bowling ball instead?

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:25.838
The ball will sink and the water level goes down.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:29.075
We'll swap for a steel ball so we guarantee it sinks.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:33.680
Just like before, we place the boat into the water and place the ball into the boat.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:40.086
Gonna need a bigger boat!
[Laughing]

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.622
It's not big enough.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.358
Nope.

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:45.992
Nope.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:49.762
It’s tipping over.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.464
There we go. 
Hey yo!

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:54.534
Woo! There we go. Just a little bit of tape.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.402
I hate experimentation.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:04:02.909
All right. After some troubleshooting, we're using a bigger boat and some tape to hold the ball in the middle so it doesn't tip over.

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.044
And bingo bango, it works.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.279
Hey yo!

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:10.350
Now we mark off our new baseline and then move the ball into the water.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.286
As you can see here, the water level went down.

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:15.188
What makes this different from before?

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.857
The ball went from floating to sinking.

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:22.795
The boat may be able to support a 16 pound bowling ball, but the water can't.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:24.597
And that matters?
Oh, yeah.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.399
It's everything.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:29.669
The “before” picture looks just like it did with the floating ball.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:33.539
Gravity pulls downward on the boat and the ball, buoyancy pushes up.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:37.910
The only difference is that this ball is heavier so the boat falls deeper into the water.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.014
The “after” picture is a bit trickier to figure out.

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:46.753
A time-honored physics tradition is to take a question like this to its extreme, just to see what happens.

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:50.923
Let's take that bowling ball and compress its mass into a smaller space.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:54.594
Say it's compressed so much that it's the size of a dust particle.

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:57.864
It's the same mass as the bowling ball, just with a tiny volume.

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:03.403
When the particle is inside the boat, its weight uses the volume of the boat to displace water.

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:07.173
But outside the boat, the particle barely displaces anything at all.

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:10.476
It would be the same for a sinking bowling ball, just less dramatic.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.846
If less water is displaced, then the water level goes down.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:16.849
Though you should be careful with solutions like this.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:22.522
First, for the comparison to work, both the bowling ball and the extreme object have to sink.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:25.258
If you were to take the bowling ball and expand it instead,

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:29.962
you would quickly make an object that would float rather than sink, breaking the comparison.

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:35.868
Another problem is that if you compress a bowling ball too much, the electrons will smash into the protons.

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:39.672
You'll get neutronium. That's the stuff neutron stars are made of.

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.708
If you put something like that into a lake,

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:44.644
it'll explode, taking the lake and you with it.

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.379
And there goes your experiment.

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:49.115
We're better off working through it without any tricks.

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:52.885
Like we said, the “before” picture looks like it did for the floating ball.

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:57.557
But this time we'll go the extra mile and solve for the displaced volume of water.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:06:02.528
You can see it depends on the mass of the boat, the mass of the ball, and the density of the water.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:05.465
This is what we're going to compare to our “after” picture.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:10.036
Once the ball falls all the way to the bottom of the lake, here's what the forces look like.

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:14.173
Just like in the floating case, each gets a weight and each gets a buoyancy.

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:18.378
However, the ball also has an upward push from the bottom of the lake.

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:21.881
Because remember, the water couldn't entirely support it.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:26.519
The water gave it a valiant effort with its buoyancy, but it just wasn't enough.

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:31.524
This upward push makes up the difference and brings the ball back into equilibrium.

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:35.828
After you work through some algebra, you'll get the new displaced volume of water.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:40.633
This big term out front is just the displaced volume for when the ball was inside the boat.

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:43.736
But now we have a second term and it's subtracted.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:49.375
That means the displaced volume after is less than the displaced volume before.

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:51.377
The water level has gone down.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:55.148
Same conclusion, no tricks, just basic principles.

00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:59.752
What about when the ball was falling through the water, though? You get a very similar result.

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:04.257
This upward push from the lake floor would just be mass times acceleration instead.

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.225
It's basically the same equation.

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:08.728
But here's the super main ultimate point:

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:12.398
Some bowling balls float on water, and some of them sink.

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:15.234
Any bowling ball 12 pounds or less will float.

00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:20.239
In which case, the water level stays the same because the water could support the ball either way.

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:23.242
Any bowling ball 13 pounds or more will sink.

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:29.649
In which case, the water level goes down because it takes more water to support the ball than it does to just let it sink.

00:07:29.649 --> 00:07:34.587
If you want to use some kind of trick to solve puzzles like this, that's fine. But you have to be careful.

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:37.457
If you're unsure, just do it the more rigorous way.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:41.427
And until next time, remember: It's okay to be a little crazy.

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:47.500
The Doctor was wondering why there are multiple bright spots in our image of a black hole.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.835
Great question!

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:51.037
Accretion disks aren't uniform.

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:55.141
These three blobs are denser parts of the disk where it's hotter.

00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:59.846
The only reason this one looks brighter than the other two is because it's moving toward us.

00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:01.247
Anyway, thanks for watching!

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:04.450
The lake demands sacrifice.

